Collections: labelled groupings of IRW tables (issue #1633).

A collection answers "give me all the tables of this type" -- RCTs, Big Five
inventories, depression scales, tables carrying a Q matrix. Tables belong to any
number of collections at once, so membership is stored long (one row per
table-collection pair), not as a column per collection.

WHY LONG FORMAT MATTERS: adding a collection is a DATA change. One line in
registry.csv, optionally one file in curated/. Nothing in ../metadata/10_collections.R
changes, and nothing in the R package, the Python package or the site changes.
A column-per-collection design would make every new collection an ~8-file change
across three repos. Do not add per-collection branches anywhere.

---------------------------------------------------------------- files

registry.csv          The spec. One row per collection, version controlled.
                      Columns: collection, label, kind, definition, rule,
                      maintainer, added.
curated/<slug>.csv    Hand-picked members for <slug>. Columns: table, basis,
                      note. Optional for rule-based collections (members are
                      unioned in); REQUIRED when rule = curated.
curated/*.review.csv  Staging only. Candidates a human has not promoted yet.
                      Never read by the pipeline -- purely a worklist.

Outputs land in ../metadata/ (collections.csv, collection_members.csv,
collections_report.txt) and are gitignored like every other pipeline output.
This directory holds the irreplaceable hand-authored input, which is why it sits
here and not under metadata/ -- src/.gitignore has `metadata/**/*.csv`.

---------------------------------------------------------------- kind

Exactly three values:

  design      how the study was run, and what structure the data carries.
              rct, clustered, q_matrix, continuous_response.
  instrument  which measure was administered. big_five, promis.
  construct   what is being measured. depression, math.

---------------------------------------------------------------- rule grammar

  var:treat                 the named variable is present
  var:date+wave             ALL named variables present            (+ = AND)
  var_any:booklet|block_id  ANY named variable present             (| = OR)
  var_prefix:qmatrix        any variable starting with the prefix
  meta:longitudinal         a logical column of metadata.csv
  cname:<regex>             case-insensitive regex over tags.csv "construct name"
  curated                   members come only from curated/<slug>.csv

Prefer `meta:` over re-deriving something metadata.csv already publishes. The
`longitudinal` collection reuses the published flag even though that flag is
loose (it greps the concatenated variable string, so cov_birthdate matches).
Two different answers to "is this table longitudinal?" would be worse than nine
loose rows.

---------------------------------------------------------------- derived fields

coverage, n_tables and basis are COMPUTED by 10_collections.R and must not be
authored in registry.csv -- authoring them is how they drift out of step with
the membership they describe.

  metadata-complete   rule read metadata.csv, so it saw every documented table.
                      NOT the same as "the whole warehouse": metadata.csv has no
                      rows for warehouses 5 and 6, and 403 tables are missing
                      from it. The script warns when configured core datasets
                      are absent.
  tagged-subset-only  rule read tags.csv, which covers 2,251 of 3,650 tables --
                      and coverage collapses in the newer warehouses (w1 99.6%,
                      w2 77.4%, w3 27.2%, w4 34.7%). These collections are
                      biased toward older warehouses and cannot honestly be
                      called "all". The denominator is appended to `definition`
                      automatically.
  curated-only        a human chose the members.

---------------------------------------------------------------- adding one

1. Add a line to registry.csv. Write the definition as the inclusion criterion
   ("what counts"), not a description of the topic.
2. If it needs hand-picked members, add curated/<slug>.csv.
3. cd ../metadata && Rscript 10_collections.R
4. Read collections_report.txt. Check the count is what you expected and, for a
   cname: rule, spot-check the matched construct names -- a broad regex is the
   easy mistake. `\bmath` catches "mathematics anxiety".
5. Upload with the site-update skill's upload_meta.py.

---------------------------------------------------------------- two invariants

1. MEMBERSHIP IS CONFIRMED-ONLY. There is no candidate/status column. Scouting
   output stays in curated/*.review.csv until a human promotes it. A published
   collection is one somebody stands behind.

2. THIS STAGE NEVER OPENS THE TAGS GOOGLE SHEET. cname: rules read the already
   published tags.csv, whose "construct name" is column 3 of 03_tags.R's
   positional KEEP_COLS. Column 4 ("Context Text" -- verbatim excerpts from
   source papers) is structurally unreachable from here, and that is a
   guarantee, not a convention. Do not "improve" the stage by reading the sheet
   directly -- any script that needs column 4 has to justify itself separately,
   and this one does not need it at all.
